 <office:body>
  <office:text text:use-soft-page-breaks="true">
   <text:sequence-decls>
    <text:sequence-decl text:display-outline-level="0" text:name="Illustration"/>
    <text:sequence-decl text:display-outline-level="0" text:name="Table"/>
    <text:sequence-decl text:display-outline-level="0" text:name="Text"/>
    <text:sequence-decl text:display-outline-level="0" text:name="Drawing"/>
    <text:sequence-decl text:display-outline-level="0" text:name="Figure"/>
   </text:sequence-decls>
   <text:p text:style-name="P12">EIRC Technology Overview</text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P13"/>
   <text:p text:style-name="P24"><text:span text:style-name="T1">The Eclipse technology consists of resource preparation, image processing research focusing on facial detection, implementation of a face processing engine, and enrollment management. In this document, intellectual property will be marked by</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T2"> </text:span><text:span text:style-name="T3"></text:span><text:span text:style-name="T5">IP</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T7"> , and unique implementations of prior art will be marked </text:span><text:span text:style-name="T10"></text:span><text:span text:style-name="T11">Key</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T7"> .</text:span></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P25"/>
   <text:p text:style-name="P26">The <text:span text:style-name="T12">Eclipse Face Processing (EFP)</text:span> <text:span text:style-name="T12">E</text:span>ngine includes the following steps:</text:p>
   <text:section text:style-name="Sect1" text:name="Section1">
    <text:list text:style-name="L1">
     <text:list-item>
      <text:p text:style-name="P47">Image Acquisition</text:p>
     </text:list-item>
     <text:list-item>
      <text:p text:style-name="P47">Frontal Face Detection</text:p>
     </text:list-item>
     <text:list-item>
      <text:p text:style-name="P47">Facial Feature (eyes) Location</text:p>
     </text:list-item>
     <text:list-item>
      <text:p text:style-name="P47">Face Image Normalization</text:p>
      <text:p text:style-name="P47"/>
     </text:list-item>
     <text:list-item>
      <text:p text:style-name="P47">Face Vector Generation</text:p>
     </text:list-item>
     <text:list-item>
      <text:p text:style-name="P47">Casual Matching</text:p>
     </text:list-item>
     <text:list-item>
      <text:p text:style-name="P47">Image Output</text:p>
     </text:list-item>
     <text:list-item>
      <text:p text:style-name="P47">Face-based Analytics</text:p>
     </text:list-item>
    </text:list>
    <text:p text:style-name="P26"/>
   </text:section>
   <text:p text:style-name="P26"/>
   <text:h text:style-name="P27" text:outline-level="1">EFP Engine</text:h>
   <text:p text:style-name="P14"/>
   <text:h text:style-name="P28" text:outline-level="2">Image Acquisition</text:h>
   <text:p text:style-name="P29">The engine acquires frame images from either a HTTP-based IP camera, such as the Axis series or a directory visible to the local machine. The ‘hot-directory’ concept <text:span text:style-name="T7"><text:s/></text:span><text:span text:style-name="T10"></text:span><text:span text:style-name="T11">Key</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T7"> </text:span><text:s/>will wait for files to be dropped into an empty directory. A loop flag allows the directory to be continuously processed for demonstration purposes. During the acquisition process, frame images are converted to a consistent RGBA32 format for output images and 8-bit greyscale for object detection purposes. Images can be scaled, typically from 4x to 0.25x to optimized the detection process. Facial recognition is optimized between 64 and 256 pixels between the eyes. But usable results are possible with 16, so the 4x scale can be helpful in those conditions. As well as quarter (or less) scale for multi megapixel headshots where the extra pixels just waste time and memory. </text:p>
   <text:h text:style-name="P28" text:outline-level="2"><text:soft-page-break/>Frontal Face Detection</text:h>
   <text:p text:style-name="P30">Face detection uses OpenCV’s Haar Cascade processing to return a list of rectangles where potential faces are detected. </text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P30">The process is roughly as follows: </text:p>
   <text:list text:style-name="L2">
    <text:list-item>
     <text:p text:style-name="P48">Load a trained Cascade Classifier. (See Face Resources below.) </text:p>
    </text:list-item>
    <text:list-item>
     <text:p text:style-name="P48">Convert a greyscale frame to OpenCV image matrix format. </text:p>
    </text:list-item>
    <text:list-item>
     <text:p text:style-name="P48">Prepare parameters for detection arguments. </text:p>
     <text:list>
      <text:list-item>
       <text:p text:style-name="P48">Scale Factor, calculated from <text:span text:style-name="Example">Min/MaxAcross</text:span>, <text:span text:style-name="Example">Min/MaxPixels</text:span>, <text:span text:style-name="Example">MaxDensity</text:span>, and <text:span text:style-name="Example">MaxDetectors</text:span>, overridden by <text:span text:style-name="Example">Factor</text:span>. </text:p>
      </text:list-item>
      <text:list-item>
       <text:p text:style-name="P48">Minimum Neighbors, calculated from <text:span text:style-name="Example">MinQuality</text:span>. <text:span text:style-name="T2"><text:s/></text:span><text:span text:style-name="T3"></text:span><text:span text:style-name="T5">IP</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T7"> </text:span></text:p>
      </text:list-item>
      <text:list-item>
       <text:p text:style-name="P48">Minimum and Maximum Size calculated from <text:span text:style-name="Example">Min/MaxAcross</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T2"> </text:span><text:span text:style-name="T3"></text:span><text:span text:style-name="T5">IP</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T7"> </text:span><text:s/>overridden by <text:span text:style-name="Example">Min/MaxPixels</text:span>. </text:p>
      </text:list-item>
      <text:list-item>
       <text:p text:style-name="P48"><text:span text:style-name="T13">The Legacy Engine groups</text:span> detections using one of three methods and their thresholds <text:span text:style-name="T13">and a</text:span>ssign<text:span text:style-name="T13">s</text:span> a face detect <text:span text:style-name="T14">quality</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T15"> </text:span><text:span text:style-name="T4"></text:span><text:span text:style-name="T6">IP</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9"> </text:span>score to each grouped set of rectangles. Note: Newer OpenCV implementations do the grouping, but our assigning quality levels is unique in the industry; others just treat it like a light switch <text:span text:style-name="T10"></text:span><text:span text:style-name="T11">Key </text:span><text:span text:style-name="T12">.</text:span></text:p>
      </text:list-item>
     </text:list>
    </text:list-item>
   </text:list>
   <text:h text:style-name="P28" text:outline-level="2">Facial Feature Location</text:h>
   <text:p text:style-name="Text_20_body">The Legacy Engine locates the left and right eyes in the Northwest and Northeast quadrants of the detected potential face. The “best” eye is used, rather than just the largest <text:span text:style-name="T10"></text:span><text:span text:style-name="T11">Key </text:span><text:span text:style-name="T7">. </text:span><text:span text:style-name="T8">If a good quality eye detection cannot be found, the face is discarded for recognition purposes. Future versions of the engine can also locate the mouth and nose and create a “T” with the eyes centered on bridge of the nose plus the nose tip and center of the mouth.</text:span></text:p>
   <text:h text:style-name="P28" text:outline-level="2">Face Image Normalization</text:h>
   <text:p text:style-name="P31">The feature (eye) locations are used to rotate and scale the face <text:span text:style-name="T17">so that the eyes match the average face used in the vector generation step below. Note that the center of the eye socket is used, not necessarily the pupil if the subject&apos;s gaze is not straight ahead.</text:span> If the normalized face image does not represent a real face, as determined by the <text:span text:style-name="Example">MinConsistency </text:span><text:span text:style-name="Example"><text:span text:style-name="T10"></text:span></text:span><text:span text:style-name="Example"><text:span text:style-name="T11">Key </text:span></text:span>parameter, that face is also discarded for facial recognition purposes.</text:p>
   <text:h text:style-name="P28" text:outline-level="2"><text:soft-page-break/>Face Vector Generation</text:h>
   <text:p text:style-name="P32"/>
   <text:h text:style-name="Heading_20_2" text:outline-level="2">Casual Matching</text:h>
   <text:p text:style-name="Text_20_body"/>
   <text:h text:style-name="Heading_20_2" text:outline-level="2">Image Output</text:h>
   <text:p text:style-name="Text_20_body"/>
   <text:h text:style-name="Heading_20_2" text:outline-level="2">Face-based Analytics</text:h>
   <text:p text:style-name="Text_20_body"/>
   <text:h text:style-name="P27" text:outline-level="1">Resources</text:h>
   <text:p text:style-name="P32"/>
   <text:h text:style-name="P28" text:outline-level="2">Face Cascade Training</text:h>
   <text:p text:style-name="P32"/>
   <text:h text:style-name="P28" text:outline-level="2">Vector Generation </text:h>
   <text:p text:style-name="P32"/>
   <text:h text:style-name="P27" text:outline-level="1">Enrollment </text:h>
   <text:p text:style-name="P32"/>
   <text:h text:style-name="P28" text:outline-level="2">Person Mode</text:h>
   <text:p text:style-name="P32"/>
   <text:h text:style-name="P27" text:outline-level="1"><text:soft-page-break/>Research</text:h>
   <text:h text:style-name="P33" text:outline-level="1">Appendix</text:h>
   <text:h text:style-name="Heading_20_2" text:outline-level="2">Haar Cascades</text:h>
   <text:p text:style-name="P34">The object detector described below has been initially proposed by Paul Viola [289] and improved by Rainer Lienhart [171] . First, a classifier (namely a cascade of boosted classifiers working with haar-like features) is trained with a few hundred sample views of a particular object (i.e., a face or a car), called positive examples, that are scaled to the same size (say, 20x20), and negative examples - arbitrary images of the same size. After a classifier is trained, it can be applied to a region of interest (of the same size as used during the training) in an input image. The classifier outputs a &quot;1&quot; if the region is likely to show the object (i.e., face/car), and &quot;0&quot; otherwise. To search for the object in the whole image one can move the search window across the image and check every location using the classifier. The classifier is designed so that it can be easily &quot;resized&quot; in order to be able to find the objects of interest at different sizes, which is more efficient than resizing the image itself. So, to find an object of an unknown size in the image the scan procedure should be done several times at different scales. The word &quot;cascade&quot; in the classifier name means that the resultant classifier consists of several simpler classifiers (stages) that are applied subsequently to a region of interest until at some stage the candidate is rejected or all the stages are passed. The word &quot;boosted&quot; means that the classifiers at every stage of the cascade are complex themselves and they are built out of basic classifiers using one of four different boosting techniques (weighted voting). Currently Discrete Adaboost, Real Adaboost, Gentle Adaboost and Logitboost are supported. The basic classifiers are decision-tree classifiers with at least 2 leaves. Haar-like features are the input to the basic classifiers, and are calculated as described below. The current algorithm uses the following Haar-like features: The feature used in a particular classifier is specified by its shape (1a, 2b etc.), position within the region of interest and the scale (this scale is not the same as the scale used at the detection stage, though these two scales are multiplied). For example, in the case of the third line feature (2c) the response is calculated as the difference between the sum of image pixels under the rectangle covering the whole feature (including the two white stripes and the black stripe in the middle) and the sum of the image pixels under the black stripe multiplied by 3 in order to compensate for the differences in the size of areas. The sums of pixel values over a rectangular regions are calculated rapidly using integral images (see below and the integral description) </text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P34"><draw:frame draw:style-name="fr1" draw:name="Frame2" text:anchor-type="char" svg:width="3.6874in" draw:z-index="2">
